{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LfXgmnSraoVSJvJwcviW1z","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd006-cat\n     2→title: \"cmd/cat: Concatenate and Display Files\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  The macOS BSD cat differs from GNU cat in flag handling (-e, -t, and -v behavior) and\n     6→  in the treatment of non-printing characters. Scripts that rely on GNU cat's -A, -v,\n     7→  -E, and -T flags for diagnostic inspection of binary or control-character content will\n     8→  behave differently on macOS. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n     9→  reference binary (gcat, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations\n    10→  including binary input, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    11→\n    12→goals:\n    13→  - G1: Concatenate one or more files (or stdin) to stdout with no transformation by default.\n    14→  - G2: Support line-numbering flags (-n, -b) that prepend line numbers to output.\n    15→  - G3: Support the blank-squeezing flag (-s) that collapses consecutive blank lines into one.\n    16→  - G4: Support non-printing display flags (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t) that make control characters and special bytes visible.\n    17→  - G5: Pass binary data through without corruption when no transformation flags are active.\n    18→\n    19→requirements:\n    20→  R1:\n    21→    title: Default behavior (no flags)\n    22→    items:\n    23→      - R1.1: Must read each named file in argument order and write its contents verbatim to stdout.\n    24→      - R1.2: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given, or when \"-\" is given as a filename.\n    25→      - R1.3: \"Must support concatenating multiple files: each file's content is written in sequence with no separator.\"\n    26→      - R1.4: \"Must not corrupt binary input: output bytes must be identical to input bytes when no transformation flags are active.\"\n    27→      - R1.5: Must not add or remove newlines from the input.\n    28→\n    29→  R2:\n    30→    title: Line numbering (-n, -b)\n    31→    items:\n    32→      - R2.1: '-n must prepend a line number to every output line. The line number must be right-justified in a field of width 6, followed by a tab character and then the line content (format: \"%6d\\t%s\"). Line numbering resets to 1 at the start of each cat invocation, not per file.'\n    33→      - R2.2: -b must prepend a line number only to non-blank lines. Blank lines receive no number and no tab prefix. -b implies -n (activates line numbering) but overrides it by skipping blank lines.\n    34→      - R2.3: When -b and -n are both given, -b takes precedence (blank lines are not numbered). This matches cat.c:602 where number_nonblank overrides number.\n    35→      - R2.4: A blank line is a line containing only a newline character (zero non-newline bytes). Lines with spaces or tabs are not blank.\n    36→\n    37→  R3:\n    38→    title: Blank-line squeezing (-s)\n    39→    items:\n    40→      - R3.1: \"-s must suppress repeated blank lines: when two or more consecutive blank lines appear in the output, only one must be written. The first blank line is written; subsequent consecutive blank lines are discarded.\"\n    41→      - R3.2: \"-s must apply across file boundaries when multiple files are concatenated: if the last line of file A and the first line of file B are both blank, they count as consecutive blank lines.\"\n    42→      - R3.3: \"-s is compatible with -n and -b. When combined, squeezing is applied before numbering: the suppressed blank lines do not consume line numbers.\"\n    43→\n    44→  R4:\n    45→    title: Non-printing display (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t)\n    46→    items:\n    47→      - R4.1: '-v must display non-printing characters using caret notation and M- prefix. Control characters (0x00-0x1F except 0x09 tab and 0x0A newline) must be shown as ^X where X is the character 64 positions higher (e.g., 0x01 -> \"^A\", 0x1B -> \"^[\"). Characters in the range 0x80-0x9F must be shown as \"M-^X\". Characters in the range 0xA0-0xFE must be shown as \"M-X\" where X is the character minus 0x80. The DEL character (0x7F) must be shown as \"^?\". 0xFF must be shown as \"M-^?\".'\n    48→      - R4.2: -v must not alter tab (0x09) or newline (0x0A) characters. Those are the two control characters exempted from -v display.\n    49→      - R4.3: -E must append a \"$\" character before each newline in the output. The newline itself is still written after the \"$\".\n    50→      - R4.4: -T must display tab characters (0x09) as the two-character sequence \"^I\" rather than the tab character itself.\n    51→      - R4.5: -A is equivalent to -v -E -T combined. All three transformations are active simultaneously.\n    52→      - R4.6: -e is equivalent to -v -E combined (show non-printing, show ends).\n    53→      - R4.7: -t is equivalent to -v -T combined (show non-printing, show tabs).\n    54→      - R4.8: '-u is accepted but has no effect (cat.c:616: \"we provide the -u feature unconditionally\"). The flag must not produce an error.'\n    55→      - R4.9: \"Transformation flags are compatible with -n, -b, and -s. The order of application is: squeeze blanks (-s), then apply non-printing display (-v/-T), then append end-of-line marker (-E), then prepend line number (-n/-b).\"\n    56→\n    57→  R5:\n    58→    title: Error handling and exit codes\n    59→    items:\n    60→      - R5.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n    61→      - R5.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened. The error message must be written to stderr. cat must continue processing remaining file arguments after the error.\n    62→      - R5.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout (e.g., SIGPIPE or full disk). The write error must be detected and reported; the process must not hang.\n    63→      - R5.4: |\n    64→          Must not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer. At startup, install a SIGPIPE handler: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Do not use signal.Ignore (deferred cleanup functions must not run on SIGPIPE, matching GNU behavior).\n    65→\n    66→non_goals:\n    67→  - cmd/cat does not implement the FIONREAD ioctl optimization from cat.c:298; it uses standard io.Copy which is sufficient for correctness.\n    68→  - cmd/cat does not implement POSIX unbuffered output semantics for -u; the flag is accepted but ignored.\n    69→  - cmd/cat does not implement terminal line-editing or interactive mode.\n    70→  - cmd/cat does not detect when input and output refer to the same file and warn about it (GNU cat does; this is a diagnostic, not a correctness requirement).\n    71→\n    72→acceptance_criteria:\n    73→  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/cat file1 file2 produces identical bytes to gcat file1 file2 for arbitrary file contents including binary.\"\n    74→  - \"AC2: printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -n produces numbered lines, matching gcat -n output byte-for-byte.\"\n    75→  - \"AC3: printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -s produces a single blank line between 'a' and 'b', matching gcat -s.\"\n    76→  - \"AC4: printf '\\\\x01\\\\x09\\\\x1b\\\\x7f\\\\x80\\\\xff' | go run ./cmd/cat -v produces the correct caret and M- notation, matching gcat -v byte-for-byte.\"\n    77→  - \"AC5: Differential test against gcat passes for all flag combinations: -n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t, and combinations thereof, on text and binary inputs.\"\n    78→  - \"AC6: go run ./cmd/cat on a non-existent file prints an error to stderr, exits 1, and still processes any other files on the command line.\"\n    79→  - \"AC7: cmd/cat compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    80→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a37955b4-bb6b-4f86-bd5e-f3601e429336","uuid":"f3875faf-9c99-49ed-8c3f-b185d4d2b9f1","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/297/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd006-cat.yaml","content":"id: prd006-cat\ntitle: \"cmd/cat: Concatenate and Display Files\"\n\nproblem: |\n  The macOS BSD cat differs from GNU cat in flag handling (-e, -t, and -v behavior) and\n  in the treatment of non-printing characters. Scripts that rely on GNU cat's -A, -v,\n  -E, and -T flags for diagnostic inspection of binary or control-character content will\n  behave differently on macOS. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n  reference binary (gcat, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations\n  including binary input, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Concatenate one or more files (or stdin) to stdout with no transformation by default.\n  - G2: Support line-numbering flags (-n, -b) that prepend line numbers to output.\n  - G3: Support the blank-squeezing flag (-s) that collapses consecutive blank lines into one.\n  - G4: Support non-printing display flags (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t) that make control characters and special bytes visible.\n  - G5: Pass binary data through without corruption when no transformation flags are active.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default behavior (no flags)\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must read each named file in argument order and write its contents verbatim to stdout.\n      - R1.2: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given, or when \"-\" is given as a filename.\n      - R1.3: \"Must support concatenating multiple files: each file's content is written in sequence with no separator.\"\n      - R1.4: \"Must not corrupt binary input: output bytes must be identical to input bytes when no transformation flags are active.\"\n      - R1.5: Must not add or remove newlines from the input.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Line numbering (-n, -b)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: '-n must prepend a line number to every output line. The line number must be right-justified in a field of width 6, followed by a tab character and then the line content (format: \"%6d\\t%s\"). Line numbering resets to 1 at the start of each cat invocation, not per file.'\n      - R2.2: -b must prepend a line number only to non-blank lines. Blank lines receive no number and no tab prefix. -b implies -n (activates line numbering) but overrides it by skipping blank lines.\n      - R2.3: When -b and -n are both given, -b takes precedence (blank lines are not numbered). This matches cat.c:602 where number_nonblank overrides number.\n      - R2.4: A blank line is a line containing only a newline character (zero non-newline bytes). Lines with spaces or tabs are not blank.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Blank-line squeezing (-s)\n    items:\n      - R3.1: \"-s must suppress repeated blank lines: when two or more consecutive blank lines appear in the output, only one must be written. The first blank line is written; subsequent consecutive blank lines are discarded.\"\n      - R3.2: \"-s must apply across file boundaries when multiple files are concatenated: if the last line of file A and the first line of file B are both blank, they count as consecutive blank lines.\"\n      - R3.3: \"-s is compatible with -n and -b. When combined, squeezing is applied before numbering: the suppressed blank lines do not consume line numbers.\"\n\n  R4:\n    title: Non-printing display (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t)\n    items:\n      - R4.1: '-v must display non-printing characters using caret notation and M- prefix. Control characters (0x00-0x1F except 0x09 tab and 0x0A newline) must be shown as ^X where X is the character 64 positions higher (e.g., 0x01 -> \"^A\", 0x1B -> \"^[\"). Characters in the range 0x80-0x9F must be shown as \"M-^X\". Characters in the range 0xA0-0xFE must be shown as \"M-X\" where X is the character minus 0x80. The DEL character (0x7F) must be shown as \"^?\". 0xFF must be shown as \"M-^?\".'\n      - R4.2: -v must not alter tab (0x09) or newline (0x0A) characters. Those are the two control characters exempted from -v display.\n      - R4.3: -E must append a \"$\" character before each newline in the output. The newline itself is still written after the \"$\".\n      - R4.4: -T must display tab characters (0x09) as the two-character sequence \"^I\" rather than the tab character itself.\n      - R4.5: -A is equivalent to -v -E -T combined. All three transformations are active simultaneously.\n      - R4.6: -e is equivalent to -v -E combined (show non-printing, show ends).\n      - R4.7: -t is equivalent to -v -T combined (show non-printing, show tabs).\n      - R4.8: '-u is accepted but has no effect (cat.c:616: \"we provide the -u feature unconditionally\"). The flag must not produce an error.'\n      - R4.9: \"Transformation flags are compatible with -n, -b, and -s. The order of application is: squeeze blanks (-s), then apply non-printing display (-v/-T), then append end-of-line marker (-E), then prepend line number (-n/-b).\"\n\n  R5:\n    title: Error handling and exit codes\n    items:\n      - R5.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R5.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened. The error message must be written to stderr. cat must continue processing remaining file arguments after the error.\n      - R5.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout (e.g., SIGPIPE or full disk). The write error must be detected and reported; the process must not hang.\n      - R5.4: |\n          Must not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer. At startup, install a SIGPIPE handler: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Do not use signal.Ignore (deferred cleanup functions must not run on SIGPIPE, matching GNU behavior).\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/cat does not implement the FIONREAD ioctl optimization from cat.c:298; it uses standard io.Copy which is sufficient for correctness.\n  - cmd/cat does not implement POSIX unbuffered output semantics for -u; the flag is accepted but ignored.\n  - cmd/cat does not implement terminal line-editing or interactive mode.\n  - cmd/cat does not detect when input and output refer to the same file and warn about it (GNU cat does; this is a diagnostic, not a correctness requirement).\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/cat file1 file2 produces identical bytes to gcat file1 file2 for arbitrary file contents including binary.\"\n  - \"AC2: printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -n produces numbered lines, matching gcat -n output byte-for-byte.\"\n  - \"AC3: printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -s produces a single blank line between 'a' and 'b', matching gcat -s.\"\n  - \"AC4: printf '\\\\x01\\\\x09\\\\x1b\\\\x7f\\\\x80\\\\xff' | go run ./cmd/cat -v produces the correct caret and M- notation, matching gcat -v byte-for-byte.\"\n  - \"AC5: Differential test against gcat passes for all flag combinations: -n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t, and combinations thereof, on text and binary inputs.\"\n  - \"AC6: go run ./cmd/cat on a non-existent file prints an error to stderr, exits 1, and still processes any other files on the command line.\"\n  - \"AC7: cmd/cat compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":80}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SbF1hDFhZP4HjnEZuy4c1W","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel01.1-uc001-cat\n     2→title: \"Concatenate and display files via cat\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator runs the Go cat binary on one or more files or stdin. The binary concatenates\n     6→  input to stdout, optionally applying line numbering, blank-line squeezing, or non-printing\n     7→  character display. The differential testing harness then runs both the Go binary and the\n     8→  Homebrew reference binary (gcat from coreutils) with the same stdin, args, and environment\n     9→  and confirms that their outputs are identical byte-for-byte. No output normalization is\n    10→  needed because cat output is deterministic.\n    11→\n    12→actor: Human Operator\n    13→\n    14→trigger: |\n    15→  The operator has completed the PRD (prd006-cat.yaml) and invokes do-work to produce\n    16→  a passing differential test for cmd/cat.\n    17→\n    18→flow:\n    19→  - id: F1\n    20→    step: |\n    21→      The operator runs the Go cat binary with file arguments or stdin.\n    22→      Example: printf 'hello\\nworld\\n' | ./bin/cat -n\n    23→  - id: F2\n    24→    step: |\n    25→      cmd/cat reads from each named file in order (or stdin when no files are given or when\n    26→      \"-\" is given). It applies the active transformations in order: squeeze blanks (-s),\n    27→      non-printing display (-v/-T), end-of-line marker (-E), line number prefix (-n/-b).\n    28→  - id: F3\n    29→    step: |\n    30→      cmd/cat writes the transformed content to stdout. With no flags, output is the\n    31→      verbatim concatenation of all inputs.\n    32→  - id: F4\n    33→    step: |\n    34→      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/cat and gcat\n    35→      with the same stdin, args, and environment.\n    36→  - id: F5\n    37→    step: |\n    38→      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte with no normalization. Both binaries must\n    39→      produce identical output bytes.\n    40→  - id: F6\n    41→    step: |\n    42→      If the outputs match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n    43→      triggering args, both outputs, and both exit codes.\n    44→\n    45→touchpoints:\n    46→  - T1: \"cmd/cat — concatenation, line numbering, blank squeezing, non-printing display (prd006-cat R1, R2, R3, R4, R5)\"\n    47→  - T2: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, binary execution and comparison\"\n    48→\n    49→success_criteria:\n    50→  - id: S1\n    51→    criterion: cmd/cat compiles and ./bin/cat exists after mage build.\n    52→  - id: S2\n    53→    criterion: |\n    54→      ./bin/cat file1 file2 produces identical bytes to gcat file1 file2 for text files.\n    55→  - id: S3\n    56→    criterion: |\n    57→      The differential test for default mode passes: ./bin/cat and gcat produce identical\n    58→      output on multi-file text input.\n    59→  - id: S4\n    60→    criterion: |\n    61→      The differential test for -n passes: line numbers match gcat output byte-for-byte\n    62→      (format, width, tab separator).\n    63→  - id: S5\n    64→    criterion: |\n    65→      The differential test for -b passes: only non-blank lines are numbered, matching\n    66→      gcat -b output.\n    67→  - id: S6\n    68→    criterion: |\n    69→      The differential test for -s passes: consecutive blank lines are collapsed to one,\n    70→      matching gcat -s output.\n    71→  - id: S7\n    72→    criterion: |\n    73→      The differential test for -v passes: non-printing characters are displayed with\n    74→      correct caret and M- notation, matching gcat -v byte-for-byte on binary input.\n    75→  - id: S8\n    76→    criterion: |\n    77→      The differential test for -A passes: combined -vET transformations match gcat -A\n    78→      byte-for-byte.\n    79→  - id: S9\n    80→    criterion: |\n    81→      The differential test for multiple files passes: files are concatenated with no\n    82→      separator, matching gcat output.\n    83→  - id: S10\n    84→    criterion: |\n    85→      A non-existent file argument produces an error to stderr, exits 1, and still\n    86→      processes other files on the command line, matching gcat behavior.\n    87→\n    88→out_of_scope:\n    89→  - FIONREAD ioctl optimization is not tested; correctness is the only requirement.\n    90→  - Same-file detection warning (cat reading from its own output) is not tested.\n    91→  - -u flag is accepted but has no observable effect; a test verifies acceptance only.\n    92→\n    93→test_suite: test-rel01.1\n    94→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a37955b4-bb6b-4f86-bd5e-f3601e429336","uuid":"03b261f5-f477-4471-816f-11558d014c76","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/297/docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.1-uc001-cat.yaml","content":"id: rel01.1-uc001-cat\ntitle: \"Concatenate and display files via cat\"\n\nsummary: |\n  The operator runs the Go cat binary on one or more files or stdin. The binary concatenates\n  input to stdout, optionally applying line numbering, blank-line squeezing, or non-printing\n  character display. The differential testing harness then runs both the Go binary and the\n  Homebrew reference binary (gcat from coreutils) with the same stdin, args, and environment\n  and confirms that their outputs are identical byte-for-byte. No output normalization is\n  needed because cat output is deterministic.\n\nactor: Human Operator\n\ntrigger: |\n  The operator has completed the PRD (prd006-cat.yaml) and invokes do-work to produce\n  a passing differential test for cmd/cat.\n\nflow:\n  - id: F1\n    step: |\n      The operator runs the Go cat binary with file arguments or stdin.\n      Example: printf 'hello\\nworld\\n' | ./bin/cat -n\n  - id: F2\n    step: |\n      cmd/cat reads from each named file in order (or stdin when no files are given or when\n      \"-\" is given). It applies the active transformations in order: squeeze blanks (-s),\n      non-printing display (-v/-T), end-of-line marker (-E), line number prefix (-n/-b).\n  - id: F3\n    step: |\n      cmd/cat writes the transformed content to stdout. With no flags, output is the\n      verbatim concatenation of all inputs.\n  - id: F4\n    step: |\n      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/cat and gcat\n      with the same stdin, args, and environment.\n  - id: F5\n    step: |\n      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte with no normalization. Both binaries must\n      produce identical output bytes.\n  - id: F6\n    step: |\n      If the outputs match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n      triggering args, both outputs, and both exit codes.\n\ntouchpoints:\n  - T1: \"cmd/cat — concatenation, line numbering, blank squeezing, non-printing display (prd006-cat R1, R2, R3, R4, R5)\"\n  - T2: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, binary execution and comparison\"\n\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - id: S1\n    criterion: cmd/cat compiles and ./bin/cat exists after mage build.\n  - id: S2\n    criterion: |\n      ./bin/cat file1 file2 produces identical bytes to gcat file1 file2 for text files.\n  - id: S3\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test for default mode passes: ./bin/cat and gcat produce identical\n      output on multi-file text input.\n  - id: S4\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test for -n passes: line numbers match gcat output byte-for-byte\n      (format, width, tab separator).\n  - id: S5\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test for -b passes: only non-blank lines are numbered, matching\n      gcat -b output.\n  - id: S6\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test for -s passes: consecutive blank lines are collapsed to one,\n      matching gcat -s output.\n  - id: S7\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test for -v passes: non-printing characters are displayed with\n      correct caret and M- notation, matching gcat -v byte-for-byte on binary input.\n  - id: S8\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test for -A passes: combined -vET transformations match gcat -A\n      byte-for-byte.\n  - id: S9\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test for multiple files passes: files are concatenated with no\n      separator, matching gcat output.\n  - id: S10\n    criterion: |\n      A non-existent file argument produces an error to stderr, exits 1, and still\n      processes other files on the command line, matching gcat behavior.\n\nout_of_scope:\n  - FIONREAD ioctl optimization is not tested; correctness is the only requirement.\n  - Same-file detection warning (cat reading from its own output) is not tested.\n  - -u flag is accepted but has no observable effect; a test verifies acceptance only.\n\ntest_suite: test-rel01.1\n","numLines":94,"startLine":1,"totalLines":94}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FBgxjWd5r8sjuoAB4QzCJ1","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n     2→// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n     3→\n     4→// Package testutils provides the differential testing harness used by cmd/\n     5→// packages to verify Go utility implementations against reference GNU binaries.\n     6→// Each test case executes two binaries with identical inputs and compares the\n     7→// Go binary's outputs against expected values.\n     8→//\n     9→// Implements: prd001-testutils\n    10→// Architecture: docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml § pkg/testutils\n    11→package testutils\n    12→\n    13→import (\n    14→\t\"bytes\"\n    15→\t\"fmt\"\n    16→\t\"os\"\n    17→\t\"os/exec\"\n    18→\t\"testing\"\n    19→)\n    20→\n    21→// DiffTest defines a single differential test case. Both the reference binary\n    22→// (refBin) and the Go binary (goBin) are executed with identical inputs; the\n    23→// Go binary's outputs are compared against the expected Want* values.\n    24→//\n    25→// Implements prd001-testutils R1.\n    26→type DiffTest struct {\n    27→\t// Args are the command-line arguments passed to both binaries.\n    28→\tArgs []string\n    29→\n    30→\t// Stdin is content piped to stdin of both binaries.\n    31→\t// nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately.\n    32→\tStdin []byte\n    33→\n    34→\t// Env contains additional KEY=VALUE environment variable pairs merged\n    35→\t// into the inherited process environment. Matching keys are overridden;\n    36→\t// new keys are added.\n    37→\tEnv []string\n    38→\n    39→\t// WantExit is the expected exit code of the Go binary.\n    40→\tWantExit int\n    41→\n    42→\t// WantStdout is the expected stdout output of the Go binary.\n    43→\tWantStdout []byte\n    44→\n    45→\t// WantStderr is the expected stderr output of the Go binary.\n    46→\tWantStderr []byte\n    47→\n    48→\t// NormStdout, when non-nil, is applied to the actual stdout before\n    49→\t// comparison, enabling normalization of acceptable differences (e.g.,\n    50→\t// stripping trailing newlines or timestamps).\n    51→\tNormStdout func([]byte) []byte\n    52→\n    53→\t// NormStderr, when non-nil, is applied to the actual stderr before\n    54→\t// comparison.\n    55→\tNormStderr func([]byte) []byte\n    56→}\n    57→\n    58→// Run executes refBin and goBin with identical inputs and reports divergence\n    59→// between the Go binary's outputs and the expected Want* values. t.Errorf is\n    60→// used (not t.Fatalf) so all three mismatch kinds — exit code, stdout, stderr\n    61→// — are reported in a single invocation without halting at the first failure.\n    62→//\n    63→// Implements prd001-testutils R2, R3.\n    64→func (tc DiffTest) Run(t *testing.T, refBin, goBin string) {\n    65→\tt.Helper()\n    66→\n    67→\tenv := append(os.Environ(), tc.Env...)\n    68→\n    69→\t// Execute the reference binary. Its output is not compared against\n    70→\t// Want*; it is run to confirm the reference binary is reachable.\n    71→\tif _, err := runBinary(refBin, tc.Args, tc.Stdin, env); err != nil {\n    72→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"reference binary %s: %v\", refBin, err)\n    73→\t}\n    74→\n    75→\t// Execute the Go binary and capture its outputs.\n    76→\tres, err := runBinary(goBin, tc.Args, tc.Stdin, env)\n    77→\tif err != nil {\n    78→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"go binary %s: %v\", goBin, err)\n    79→\t}\n    80→\n    81→\tgotStdout := res.stdout\n    82→\tgotStderr := res.stderr\n    83→\tif tc.NormStdout != nil {\n    84→\t\tgotStdout = tc.NormStdout(gotStdout)\n    85→\t}\n    86→\tif tc.NormStderr != nil {\n    87→\t\tgotStderr = tc.NormStderr(gotStderr)\n    88→\t}\n    89→\n    90→\t// R3: report each mismatch independently so all failures are visible.\n    91→\tif res.exitCode != tc.WantExit {\n    92→\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s: exit code = %d, want %d\", goBin, res.exitCode, tc.WantExit)\n    93→\t}\n    94→\tif !bytes.Equal(gotStdout, tc.WantStdout) {\n    95→\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s: stdout = %q, want %q\", goBin, gotStdout, tc.WantStdout)\n    96→\t}\n    97→\tif !bytes.Equal(gotStderr, tc.WantStderr) {\n    98→\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s: stderr = %q, want %q\", goBin, gotStderr, tc.WantStderr)\n    99→\t}\n   100→}\n   101→\n   102→// binaryResult holds the captured output of a single binary execution.\n   103→type binaryResult struct {\n   104→\tstdout   []byte\n   105→\tstderr   []byte\n   106→\texitCode int\n   107→}\n   108→\n   109→// runBinary executes the binary at path with the given arguments, stdin, and\n   110→// environment and returns the captured stdout, stderr, and exit code.\n   111→// It returns an error only when the binary cannot be started; a non-zero exit\n   112→// code is captured in the result, not treated as an error.\n   113→func runBinary(path string, args []string, stdin []byte, env []string) (binaryResult, error) {\n   114→\tvar outBuf, errBuf bytes.Buffer\n   115→\tcmd := exec.Command(path, args...)\n   116→\tcmd.Stdin = bytes.NewReader(stdin)\n   117→\tcmd.Stdout = &outBuf\n   118→\tcmd.Stderr = &errBuf\n   119→\tcmd.Env = env\n   120→\n   121→\terr := cmd.Run()\n   122→\texitCode := 0\n   123→\tif err != nil {\n   124→\t\texitErr, ok := err.(*exec.ExitError) //nolint:errorlint\n   125→\t\tif !ok {\n   126→\t\t\treturn binaryResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"starting %s: %w\", path, err)\n   127→\t\t}\n   128→\t\texitCode = exitErr.ExitCode()\n   129→\t}\n   130→\treturn binaryResult{\n   131→\t\tstdout:   outBuf.Bytes(),\n   132→\t\tstderr:   errBuf.Bytes(),\n   133→\t\texitCode: exitCode,\n   134→\t}, nil\n   135→}\n   136→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a37955b4-bb6b-4f86-bd5e-f3601e429336","uuid":"4bc7f6eb-f2af-4dd8-9ab1-93fb55747b0d","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/297/pkg/testutils/difftest.go","content":"// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n\n// Package testutils provides the differential testing harness used by cmd/\n// packages to verify Go utility implementations against reference GNU binaries.\n// Each test case executes two binaries with identical inputs and compares the\n// Go binary's outputs against expected values.\n//\n// Implements: prd001-testutils\n// Architecture: docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml § pkg/testutils\npackage testutils\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\n// DiffTest defines a single differential test case. Both the reference binary\n// (refBin) and the Go binary (goBin) are executed with identical inputs; the\n// Go binary's outputs are compared against the expected Want* values.\n//\n// Implements prd001-testutils R1.\ntype DiffTest struct {\n\t// Args are the command-line arguments passed to both binaries.\n\tArgs []string\n\n\t// Stdin is content piped to stdin of both binaries.\n\t// nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately.\n\tStdin []byte\n\n\t// Env contains additional KEY=VALUE environment variable pairs merged\n\t// into the inherited process environment. Matching keys are overridden;\n\t// new keys are added.\n\tEnv []string\n\n\t// WantExit is the expected exit code of the Go binary.\n\tWantExit int\n\n\t// WantStdout is the expected stdout output of the Go binary.\n\tWantStdout []byte\n\n\t// WantStderr is the expected stderr output of the Go binary.\n\tWantStderr []byte\n\n\t// NormStdout, when non-nil, is applied to the actual stdout before\n\t// comparison, enabling normalization of acceptable differences (e.g.,\n\t// stripping trailing newlines or timestamps).\n\tNormStdout func([]byte) []byte\n\n\t// NormStderr, when non-nil, is applied to the actual stderr before\n\t// comparison.\n\tNormStderr func([]byte) []byte\n}\n\n// Run executes refBin and goBin with identical inputs and reports divergence\n// between the Go binary's outputs and the expected Want* values. t.Errorf is\n// used (not t.Fatalf) so all three mismatch kinds — exit code, stdout, stderr\n// — are reported in a single invocation without halting at the first failure.\n//\n// Implements prd001-testutils R2, R3.\nfunc (tc DiffTest) Run(t *testing.T, refBin, goBin string) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\n\tenv := append(os.Environ(), tc.Env...)\n\n\t// Execute the reference binary. Its output is not compared against\n\t// Want*; it is run to confirm the reference binary is reachable.\n\tif _, err := runBinary(refBin, tc.Args, tc.Stdin, env); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"reference binary %s: %v\", refBin, err)\n\t}\n\n\t// Execute the Go binary and capture its outputs.\n\tres, err := runBinary(goBin, tc.Args, tc.Stdin, env)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"go binary %s: %v\", goBin, err)\n\t}\n\n\tgotStdout := res.stdout\n\tgotStderr := res.stderr\n\tif tc.NormStdout != nil {\n\t\tgotStdout = tc.NormStdout(gotStdout)\n\t}\n\tif tc.NormStderr != nil {\n\t\tgotStderr = tc.NormStderr(gotStderr)\n\t}\n\n\t// R3: report each mismatch independently so all failures are visible.\n\tif res.exitCode != tc.WantExit {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s: exit code = %d, want %d\", goBin, res.exitCode, tc.WantExit)\n\t}\n\tif !bytes.Equal(gotStdout, tc.WantStdout) {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s: stdout = %q, want %q\", goBin, gotStdout, tc.WantStdout)\n\t}\n\tif !bytes.Equal(gotStderr, tc.WantStderr) {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s: stderr = %q, want %q\", goBin, gotStderr, tc.WantStderr)\n\t}\n}\n\n// binaryResult holds the captured output of a single binary execution.\ntype binaryResult struct {\n\tstdout   []byte\n\tstderr   []byte\n\texitCode int\n}\n\n// runBinary executes the binary at path with the given arguments, stdin, and\n// environment and returns the captured stdout, stderr, and exit code.\n// It returns an error only when the binary cannot be started; a non-zero exit\n// code is captured in the result, not treated as an error.\nfunc runBinary(path string, args []string, stdin []byte, env []string) (binaryResult, error) {\n\tvar outBuf, errBuf bytes.Buffer\n\tcmd := exec.Command(path, args...)\n\tcmd.Stdin = bytes.NewReader(stdin)\n\tcmd.Stdout = &outBuf\n\tcmd.Stderr = &errBuf\n\tcmd.Env = env\n\n\terr := cmd.Run()\n\texitCode := 0\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\texitErr, ok := err.(*exec.ExitError) //nolint:errorlint\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\treturn binaryResult{}, fmt.Errorf(\"starting %s: %w\", path, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\texitCode = exitErr.ExitCode()\n\t}\n\treturn binaryResult{\n\t\tstdout:   outBuf.Bytes(),\n\t\tstderr:   errBuf.Bytes(),\n\t\texitCode: exitCode,\n\t}, nil\n}\n","numLines":136,"startLine":1,"totalLines":136}}}
