le2 → covered by TestCatMultipleFiles ✓\n- AC2: -n → covered by TestCatLineNumberingN ✓\n- AC3: -s → covered by TestCatSqueezeBlanks ✓\n- AC4: -v with binary → covered by TestCatShowNonprinting ✓\n- AC5: All flag combinations → covered individually ✓\n- AC6: non-existent file → covered by TestCatMissingFile ✓\n\nNow I'm verifying that all the individual flags are tested: -n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t, and -u each have their own test cases, plus I've got coverage for the -b -n combination from R2.3.\n\nI should also add `t.Parallel()` to all test functions since they're safe to run concurrently—they only use `t.TempDir()`, spawn isolated subprocesses, and don't mutate any global state.\n\nFor the `buildGoBinary` function, I need to handle the working directory carefully when `go build` runs. Go test sets the cwd to the package directory, so using the relative path `../../bin/cat` should work fine as long as I trust that standard behavior.\n\nNow I'm verifying the test cases for the squeeze blanks and combined flags features, checking that the input gets processed correctly and produces the expected output.\n\nWriting the test file... I'm working through the test case for `cat_show_nonprinting`, where the YAML representation `\\\\x09` actually decodes to a literal tab character, so the expected output should be `^A` followed by a tab, then `^[`, `^?`, and the multi-byte character sequences.\n\nNow I'm thinking about how to structure the test file itself—I could use a table-driven approach for the stdin-based tests to keep things organized and follow Go conventions, rather than writing separate test functions for each case. I think the table-driven approach works well for stdin tests, but file-based tests need their own functions since they require temporary directory setup. So I'll organize it as separate test functions for each file scenario rather than trying to combine them in one table.\n\nLooking back at the constraints, I see I shouldn't be reading files from the filesystem to infer style or patterns—everything I need is already in the provided context. The project context includes the test utilities and specifications, so I have what I need. Since the working branch only has specifications and no existing test files to reference, I'll just proceed with writing the test file from scratch following the style guide.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the complete test file. I've settled on using table-driven tests for the stdin cases under a main `TestCat` function, separate test functions for filesystem operations, package-level variables for the binaries set up in `TestMain`, a helper to build the Go binary in a `bin/` directory, and logic to find the reference binary (trying gcat first, then cat). All tests will run in parallel, the error case will use `NormStderr` to verify the filename appears in the error output, and I'll set `LC_ALL=C` across all test environments.\n\nLet me verify the `NormStderr` function works as expected for the missing file test...\n\nActually, I'm realizing the stderr output from the binary will include the full error message with the path, so I need to make sure the closure properly captures the `missingPath` variable and the normalization function correctly identifies when that path appears in the error output.\n\nFor the `-b -n` flag combination test, I'm checking how the implementation handles the `number` configuration option.  When both flags are provided, the blank line numbering behavior takes precedence, so blank lines don't get numbered—they just appear as empty lines. My test case verifies this with input containing a blank line and expects the output to show numbered non-blank lines with the blank line untouched. Now I'm deciding on the test structure: table-driven tests work well for the stdin cases since they're similar, but the file-based tests are distinct enough that individual test functions make more sense.\n\nFor the binary path, I need to handle the working directory issue carefully. Building a helper function to construct an absolute path...\n\nWriting the build helper...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15156,"cache_read_input_tokens":55802,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15156},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a37955b4-bb6b-4f86-bd5e-f3601e429336","uuid":"496bffaa-c914-4a75-b64f-a1f20a60c114"}
